Chronic Low Back Pain
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lumbosacral pain >12 weeks, Mechanical exacerbation, Paraspinal tenderness, Normal lower limb neurology

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-013
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute-on-chronic exacerbation of unremitting lumbosacral pain causing profound immobility and psychological distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with chronic low back pain experiencing a sudden, severe, and functionally paralyzing flare-up that was entirely unresponsive to their usual analgesic regimen. Urgent physical and neurological assessment was required to definitively exclude new acute red flags (e.g., cauda equina syndrome, osteomyelitis) and to implement immediate, escalated pain modulation to break a severe pain-spasm cycle prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My usual back pain just went into overdrive; my muscles have seized up into rocks, and I can't even stand to make a cup of tea."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a 5-year history of fluctuating dull lumbosacral pain presents with an acute, severe exacerbation. Timeline: Baseline persistent pain localized between the lower rib cage and gluteal folds; suddenly worsened 12 hours ago after prolonged standing at a family event. Pain is currently a severe, throbbing ache exacerbated by any minor mechanical loading (shifting weight, attempting to stand). Patient reports severe morning-style stiffness that has failed to improve with movement today. Disrupted sleep architecture noted over the past week, culminating in extreme fatigue today. No subjective bowel or bladder changes.
Functional Status: Temporarily bed-bound; able to log-roll but unable to load the spine vertically.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound fatigue and psychological distress secondary to unrelenting pain and functional loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, grimacing with any truncal movement.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of both lower extremities is completely normal: 5/5 motor strength, intact sensation to light touch/pinprick, symmetrical 2+ patellar and Achilles reflexes. Plantar responses flexor. Negative straight leg raise bilaterally. Significant palpable tenderness and hypertonicity in the lumbar paraspinal musculature and bilateral quadratus lumborum. Active lumbar range of motion heavily restricted in flexion and extension by mechanical pain, not nerve tension. No focal midline bony tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty blister packs of standard OTC analgesics on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has maximized regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen doses over the last 24 hours with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical exacerbation of Chronic Low Back Pain - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out by definitively intact lower extremity neurology, normal reflexes, and absence of saddle anesthesia or sphincter dysfunction.

Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy (Sciatica) - Ruled out by the negative straight leg raise and absence of pain radiating past the gluteal fold into the distal leg.

Vertebral Osteomyelitis - Ruled out by the absence of systemic fever, lack of focal point bony tenderness, and a clear mechanical trigger for the exacerbation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing acute flare of established chronic mechanical low back pain. The critical imperative was to physically assess the patient to rule out new, emergent neurological complications (red flags) that can mimic a simple flare. After confirming a completely normal lower limb neurological examination, it was determined that the crisis was driven by a severe, refractory paraspinal muscle spasm cycle. The patient had exhausted their home analgesic options and was functionally paralyzed by pain. Postponing intervention until morning would have left the patient in an unsafe, immobilized state with worsening distress. Urgent after-hours care safely provided a higher tier of targeted pharmacological relief to unlock the musculature and restore basic functional mobility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed a short 3-day course of oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN to explicitly target the paraspinal hypertonicity.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Transition from the bed to a firm chair for 10 minutes every two hours, focusing on deep diaphragmatic breathing. Consequence: Gentle, progressive loading of the spine prevents secondary deconditioning and stiffness, making the next movement attempt less jarring (positive reinforcement).
Action: Apply a structured heat pack to the lower back before any attempted transfer. Consequence: Increased local vascularity softens the rigid paraspinal muscles, immediately reducing the sharp, catching pain when standing up (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to reassess baseline pain management strategy and arrange physiotherapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of bowel or bladder incontinence, numbness in the groin, or new, severe shooting pain or weakness down the legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis represents a flare of a chronic mechanical condition. Formal imaging is generally not indicated for acute flares without neurological signs. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute muscle spasm will recede over 3-7 days with targeted therapy, returning you to your baseline. Prolonged bed rest is strictly contraindicated as it rapidly worsens chronic back pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you lose control of your bladder or bowels, or if your legs become suddenly weak and numb.

